    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The debate over the Second Amendment often centers on the conflict between **originalism** and **living constitutionalism**.\n\n**Originalism** generally argues that the amendment should be interpreted based on the understanding of the text and its context at the time of its adoption, favoring a more restrictive view on gun rights for certain uses. **Living constitutionalism**, in contrast, believes the Constitution is a \"living document\" that must evolve to address modern societal needs and technological changes, often supporting a broader and more robust right to self-defense in the 21st century.",
      "source": "target",
